    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d801">

                <pb facs="0907" n="795" sameAs="frus:frus1935v03/pg795"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">693.002/187</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Minister in China</hi>
                        (<hi rendition="#italic">Johnson</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 1612</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1935-03-25">March 25, 1935</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: Referring to despatches Nos. 18 and 21 of
                    February 2 and 9, 1935, respectively, from the American Consulate at
                        Tsingtao,<anchor n="6" xml:id="d801-f1"/> copies of which were, according to
                    notations contained thereon, forwarded to the Legation, on the subject of the
                    position of Americans in the Chinese Maritime Customs Service, the Department
                    desires that the Legation submit a report giving its views in regard to the
                    above matter in the light of the statements contained in these despatches.</p>
                <p>Very truly yours,</p>
                <closer>For the Secretary of State:<lb/><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">William
                            Phillips</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
